<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 1px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is My Favorite. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Favorite.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Describe a current personal experience in detail in a logical sequence. interactive: Engage in simple exchanges about personal interests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a current personal experience related to familiar places in sequence when sharing with peers (e.g., 'I am at the park, and I see trees.'). Can engage in simple exchanges about personal interests and listen to peers' preferences (e.g., 'I like the park because it is fun. What do you like?'). Can express opinions about a familiar place (e.g., 'The park is the best because it has swings.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various large picture cards of neighborhood places (park, street, playground, building, tree, flower). Students identify the places chorally and individually. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages simple responses like "A park!" or "A tree!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture of a park. "This is a park. I like the park because it is fun. It is the best place to play. My favorite is the park." The teacher emphasizes the sounds /b/ in 'best' and /f/ in 'favorite'. The teacher models blending onset /b/ and rhyme /ɪg/ to form 'big,' then 'bigger,' 'biggest' with gestures. Then, the teacher models the question, "What is your favorite place?" and answers with a complete sentence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a park) "Is this park my favorite?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I like the park because it is boring?" (Expected answer: No! Because it is fun!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Start Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[End Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short illustrated dialogue between two friends, Leo and Mia, taking "A Walk in the Neighborhood," expressing preferences and opinions. The genre is a descriptive dialogue, register is informal, and format is a picture story with speech bubbles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand and express simple preferences and opinions about familiar places and activities in their neighborhood.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Excerpt:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "Look, Mia! This is a beautiful flower! It is the best flower on the street."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mia: "Yes, it is! I like to walk on this path because it is quiet. My favorite place is the playground because it is fun."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "I enjoy the park. I see big trees. The park is the best because I can run and play."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mia: "What is your favorite part of the park?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Leo and Mia talking about?" (Expected: Favorite places in the neighborhood).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Mia likes the path because it is noisy." (False, it is quiet). "Leo thinks the park is the best because he can run." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Match characters (Leo, Mia) to their favorite places (park, playground) and reasons (fun, run, play, quiet).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Start Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[End Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice describing preferences and expressing opinions using superlatives ('best', 'worst'), simple present + 'because', and question structures ('What is your favorite?'). Vocabulary includes: neighborhood, park, place, tree, flower, street, playground, walk, like, enjoy, fun, exciting, beautiful, favorite. Emphasis on /b/ and /f/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion (Oral).</b> The teacher provides sentence starters on the board or flashcards. In pairs, students orally complete the sentences using target vocabulary. E.g., "The _______ is the _______ because it is ______." (e.g., The *park* is the *best* because it is *fun*). "I like the _______ because ______." (e.g., I like the *street* because it has *beautiful trees*). The teacher monitors for correct use of superlatives and 'because', and clear pronunciation of /b/ and /f/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Picture Prompt & Substitution Drill.</b> The teacher shows different pictures of neighborhood elements (e.g., a *big* building, a *small* flower, a *busy* street, a *quiet* path). For each picture, students in pairs practice forming sentences: "This [place] is [adjective]." "It is the [superlative] [place]." "I like it because it is [reason]." The teacher guides with sentence frames like: "This is a ______ (big) tree. It is the ______ (best) tree because it is ______ (tall)." Students take turns saying their sentences aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Ask & Share."</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives 3-4 picture cards of different neighborhood places. Student A asks, "What is your favorite place?" Student B responds, "My favorite place is the [place] because it is [adjective]." Student A then asks, "What do you like to do there?" Student B answers (e.g., "I like to play!"). They swap roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation as needed, especially for /b/ and /f/ sounds.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Start Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[End Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Neighborhood Tour" (Role-play & Presentation).</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Each pair creates a simple "map" or sequence of 3-4 drawings representing a small section of a neighborhood walk. They mark 2-3 "favorite" places on their map/drawing (e.g., park, street, playground, building with flowers). One student acts as the "tour guide," describing the walk and their favorite places, giving reasons. The other student acts as the "visitor," listening and asking simple follow-up questions about preferences. After 3-4 minutes, they switch roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Instructions for students:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Imagine you are taking a friend on a walk in your neighborhood."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a simple path and 3-4 places you see."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Tell your friend about your walk and describe your favorite places. Use 'I see a...', 'I like the [place] because it is [adjective]', 'This is the best [place] because [reason]', and ask 'What is your favorite?'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The friend listens and asks questions about what they like."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The activity focuses on encouraging fluid conversation and expressing opinions related to the theme "This Is My Favorite." within the scenario "A Walk in the Neighborhood."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Start Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[End Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Neighborhood Tour" activity, combined with a quick peer-assessment for effort.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "My Neighborhood Tour" role-play in pairs for the teacher or another pair.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Speaks clearly, including /b/ in 'best' and /f/ in 'favorite'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 3-4 vocabulary words (e.g., park, street, flower, fun, beautiful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Successfully uses 'This is my favorite...', 'I like... because...', and superlatives ('the best').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Sequence:</b> Describes the walk in a logical, understandable sequence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Engages with their partner by asking/answering questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to talk about my favorite ______________ because ______________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is ______________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Start Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[End Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of describing your favorite place was easy for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult, and what could help you do better next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., specific activities, student engagement, use of target language).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with expressing opinions or using 'because'? (e.g., more sentence frames, specific pronunciation drills for /b/ and /f/, review of vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did students manage the blend of spoken simple onsets and rhymes (onset /b/ and rhyme /ɪg/) in words like 'big' and 'biggest'?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about favorite places and giving reasons. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will build on this by having students write simple sentences and short paragraphs about their favorite neighborhood places, further solidifying the vocabulary, grammar, and pragmatic functions practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Start Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[End Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>